# base.pkgs — what every Ember image has, on every architecture.
#
# Void's own base-system pulls a kernel and firmware, which the Pi profiles
# replace with their own; base-minimal plus the pieces named here is the
# smallest thing that still boots, logs in and reaches a network.
base-minimal
# ⛔ eudev, EXPLICITLY. base-minimal pulls eudev-libudev — the LIBRARY — and not
# the daemon, so a system built from it runs NO udev at all. Nothing then tags
# devices, seat0 gets no devices, elogind reports the seat as non-graphical, and
# lightdm waits for ever for a seat that never becomes usable: a machine that
# boots to a console and never starts X, with nothing anywhere saying why.
# Separately it means no modalias autoloading, which is why the Pi's wifi and
# display drivers never loaded on their own either.
eudev
runit-void
dhcpcd
iproute2
iputils
wpa_supplicant
openssh
ca-certificates
util-linux
e2fsprogs
dosfstools
# ⚠ sudo is BASE, not a desktop extra. The image creates a wheel user and drops
# a rule into /etc/sudoers.d — and that directory belongs to the sudo package,
# so without it the image build fails on "No such file or directory" and a
# machine that did build would have an administrative user who cannot administer
# anything. A sudoers rule for an uninstalled package is not a small mistake.
# growpart, for expanding the root partition on first boot after the image has
# been written to a card larger than itself. It is built for growing a MOUNTED
# partition, which is what the running root is.
cloud-guest-utils
# ⚠ lspci. On hardware this old, identifying the host bridge and the graphics
# card is the first step of nearly every diagnosis, and its absence cost a round
# trip chasing an AGP fallback on the Pentium 4.
pciutils
usbutils
sudo
nano
less
which
xbps
